Some field information has no form field: why a site was chosen, how the forest
looked, who fell ill, what puzzled the collector. NAHPU keeps it in
**narratives**, a dated journal in which each entry links to a site and a
writer.

Heru's 2014 journal is full of this kind of information. It is also written in
the English of someone who failed two English tests that year. Before entering
it, correct the language without changing the facts.

## How to correct a field journal

- **Keep every fact.** Counts, times, places, names, and observations stay as
  written, even when they seem unimportant.
- **Fix the language.** Correct grammar, tense, plurals, and word choice. Split
  run-on sentences.
- **Fix names, and say so.** Correct a misspelled or outdated scientific name
  and keep the original in brackets the first time.
- **Do not resolve conflicts by editing.** If the journal and a specimen form
  disagree, enter both as written and explain the conflict in the specimen
  record, as you did in [Basic tutorial 5](/en/tutorials/basic/specimens/).
- **Keep the scan.** Attach the scanned page to the entry so anyone can check
  the original.

## 1. Compare one entry

This is the entry for 12 September 2014 in Heru's journal. The teammates'
names are redacted.

![Handwritten journal entry for 12 September 2014 with the teammates' names blacked out](../../../../../assets/images/tutorials/basic/journal-2014-09-12.jpg)

Transcribed as written, with the teammates' names replaced:

```text
Bima, Citra, the guide, and I set 10 pitfalls around 50 meters from the camp.
then I set 31 snap traps next to the pitfalls. Both pitfalls and snap traps
called Line 1. Citra and Bima set 25 snap traps in other side called Line 2.
I got 1 squirrels right after I set the traps. Forest type almost similar all
line, which is slightly disturbed caused by hiker activities.
```

The corrected version:

```text
Bima, Citra, Eko (our guide), and I set 10 pitfall traps about 50 m from the
camp. I then set 31 snap traps next to the pitfalls; together they form
Line 1. Citra and Bima set 25 snap traps on the other side of the camp as
Line 2. I caught a squirrel right after setting the traps. The forest is
similar along all the lines and slightly disturbed by hikers.
```

Every number and place survives. The squirrel is HH 436, caught at 16:00 the
same day.

## 2. Enter the entries

{% steps %}

1. Open Narratives and select `+`.
2. Enter the `Date`, choose `Heru Handika` as `Writer`, and choose the `Site ID`
   from the table below.
3. Paste the corrected text. Narratives accept Markdown, so you can italicize
   scientific names with underscores, such as `_Maxomys hylomyoides_`.
4. Attach the scanned journal page as media.
5. Select `Preview` to check the rendered text.

{% /steps %}

| Date | `Site ID` | Why this site |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 12 Sep 2014 | `TJHH-L1` | Lines 1 and 2 set |
| 13 Sep 2014 | `TJHH-L1` | First trap check at Pondok Merah |
| 16 Sep 2014 | `TJHH-L3` | Move to Pasir Putih; Lines 3–5 set |
| 17 Sep 2014 | `TJHH-L3` | First trap check at Pasir Putih |
| 18 Sep 2014 | `TJHH-L3` | Lines pulled; the possible new *Maxomys* |

A narrative links to one site. For days that cover several lines, choose the
main one and name the others in the text.

This is the 18 September entry in preview mode, linked to `TJHH-L3`. The
`Time` field stays empty because the journal gives no time for the entry:

![NAHPU Narrative page on an iPad for 18 September 2014, site TJHH-L3, writer Heru Handika, with the corrected entry rendered and Maxomys hylomyoides in italics](../../../../../assets/images/app-screenshots/tutorials/basic/narrative-2014-09-18.webp)

## 3. The corrected entries

### 13 September 2014

```markdown
Line 1 produced 14 rats and 6 shrews; all the shrews came from the pitfalls.
Line 2 produced 25 rats. Some traps caught more than one animal in a night,
because we rebaited them after each capture. The night temperature fell from
about 16 °C to 14 °C. There was no rain; the sky was sometimes cloudy and
sometimes very clear.
```

The rat count for Line 1 is overwritten on paper and reads as 14. The weather
is also in the `TJHH-L1` event's environmental data, where it can be searched
and exported.

### 16 September 2014

```markdown
Line 1 produced 3 shrews and 1 rat; Line 2 produced nothing. We hired a
fisherman, Fajar, to take us across the lake by wooden boat. Eko (our guide),
Fajar, Bima, Dian, and I took a 1.5-hour boat ride from the camp to a site
called Pasir Putih.

The new site was nearly pristine, with very little sign of human activity,
unlike the forest near the camp, which hikers have slightly disturbed. On the
way we counted five fishermen's shelters at three places, all beside the lake.

Bima and I set 20 snap traps at the first suitable place we found in Pasir
Putih. The forest there is nearly pristine. I saw only one trail, and it seemed
rarely used. There are many big trees for a montane forest, 1–2 m in diameter,
and most are covered in moss. The ground is fairly open, with few small plants.
_Elephantopus_ (an invasive Asteraceae) along the lake edge hides the forest
behind it.

We moved to a second place close to the first and set pitfalls. The forest here
is more open and the ground cover denser, but it looks intact. We think natural
processes opened the canopy; there are also fewer big trees than at the first
place.

At the last place we set 20 more snap traps, next to the pitfalls. This forest
differs from the other two: open in some areas and closed in others, with many
lianas. Bima and I set many of the traps on lianas. The moss cover on the trees
is similar to the first place. Because the three places are well separated and
have different forest conditions, I named the first trap line Line 3, the
pitfall line Line 4, and the last line Line 5.

While setting the traps, I heard siamangs (_Symphalangus syndactylus_; written
in the field as _Hylobates syndactylus_) close by. We also found edelweiss near
the site. We left camp at 09:45 and returned at 16:30.

I felt sick when we got back and had to lie down before preparing the
specimens. Overall, the team was still happy and enjoying camp life after five
nights at high elevation. One thing I don't understand is why we caught so few
birds compared with mammals, even compared with the average of the surveys we
have done so far.

Bima and Citra set most of the Line 2 snap traps above the ground, about
1–2 m high.
```

The descriptions of the three places are also in the habitat of `TJHH-L3`,
`TJHH-P4`, and `TJHH-L5` (Tutorial 3). The journal keeps the full account; the
sites keep the part each record needs.

### 17 September 2014

```markdown
Line 2 produced 2 _Chiropodomys_, 2 _Niviventer_, and 1 _Maxomys_. Bima,
Citra, Eko, Dian, and Fajar checked and rebaited the Pasir Putih traps by boat,
while I prepared some of the rats in camp. They left at 08:00 and returned at
13:15 with 5 rats and 1 gymnure from Line 3, 6 shrews and 1 young gymnure from
Line 4, and 4 rats from Line 5.
```

This entry explains two choices in Tutorial 5: why HH 523 and HH 529 have
`Bima Sampel` as collector, and why their times are `Morning` rather than the
07:00 and 09:00 on the forms. Two counts are overwritten on paper and read as
1 gymnure and 6 shrews.

### 18 September 2014

```markdown
Line 2 produced 1 squirrel and 1 rat; Line 3, 5 rats and 2 gymnures; Line 4,
4 shrews; and Line 5, 3 rats. Citra, Dian, Eko, Fajar, and I pulled all the
pitfalls and snap traps from Lines 3, 4, and 5, and the mist net as well.

The survey at this new site, far from human access, added one shrew species
and what may be a new rat. The rat looks like _Maxomys hylomyoides_, but its
underparts are darker: more orange at the base and grey on the belly. We had
only one line set up.
```

This is the context for HH 523. Its paper remark, `Underpart darker than
M. hylomyoides`, makes full sense only next to this entry. In NAHPU, the
specimen and the entry share the site `TJHH-L3`, so a reader of either can find
the other. On paper, HH 523's sheet has no locality, and the journal is a
separate notebook.

{% aside type="note" %}
The journal mentions a mist net at Pasir Putih, but Heru's catalog has no birds
or bats from it. The team also included an ornithologist. To keep the focus on
the mammal catalog, the bird work is not included in this tutorial.
{% /aside %}

## What you gained over paper

- The journal reads clearly, with every fact intact and the scan attached.
- Each entry is dated and linked to a site and a writer.
- Specimen records and journal entries explain each other: HH 436 and the
  squirrel on 12 September, HH 523 and the possible new rat on 18 September.

